pseudo-Boolean proof version 2.0
* Regression test: `left` and `right` name the two sides of the same relation
* and must be the same length. `OrderRightVariablesRule` checks that when
* `right` is parsed, which covers version 3.0 (whose grammar admits each list
* once, in that order), but the version 2.0 parser dispatches line by line and
* used to accept a SECOND, longer `left` afterwards. The order below was then
* built with left = [u2, u3] and right = [v1], and every consumer --
* `ActiveOrder::new`, `get_reflexivity_substitution`,
* `get_transitivity_substitution`, `get_proofgoals_strict` -- zips the two
* lists with `izip!` and silently truncates, each to a different length, while
* `load_order` sizes its literal list against `left` alone. Found by the sweep
* that followed the `fresh_right` defect (see
* incorrect/version3/def_order_transitivity_fresh_right_empty): the same shape,
* a list trusted to have a length nothing establishes. The symmetry is now
* re-checked when the `vars` block closes.
f 1
pre_order bad
    vars
        left u1
        right v1
        left u2 u3
        aux
    end
    def
        1 ~u2 1 v1 >= 1 ;
    end
    transitivity
        vars
            fresh_right w1
        end
        proof
            proofgoal #1
                u >= 1 ;
            end -1
        end
    end
end
load_order bad x1 x2
output NONE
conclusion NONE
end pseudo-Boolean proof
